## A task:  something to do -- in the outline style:  its name, then what's true of it
// A view:  each task is its own element on the page, `<task-list-task>`.
<spell-view "task">
	<property "title">text</>
	<property "notes">text</>
	<property "priority" one-of="low, normal or high" />
	<property "done">yes or no</>
	<alias "is done">if its done is yes</>	// "task is done"
	<alias "is active">if its done is no</>	// "task is active"
	<property "priority color" description="its priority label's color:  red when it's high">
		red if its priority is high
		grey otherwise
	</property>

	// on the page, a click selects it;  shift and command (ctrl) select more,
	// so the property editor beside the list changes them all at once
	<spell>
		- it can be selected
		// and dragged, by its grip, to another place in its list:  the selected ones go with it
		- it can be dragged

		## drawing:  its grip, a checkbox, its title, and its priority -- styles.css shows it only when it isn't normal
		// a form of its own, bound to it:  checking the box, or typing a title, changes the task
		// - its row and its title are parts:  styles.css lays them out
		- to draw it:
			return <ui-form value={it}>
				<div part="row">
					<ui-icon part="grip" name="grip vertical" aria-label="Drag"/>
					<ui-checkbox name="done" aria-label="Done"/>
					<ui-input part="title" name="title" aria-label="Task" transparent/>
					<ui-label part="priority" size="tiny" color={its priority color}>{its priority}</ui-label>
				</div>
			</ui-form>
	</spell>
</spell-view>


## Tests
<spell>
	to test tasks
		set the task to a new task with title = "Write the tests"
		the task is active should be yes
		set the done of the task to yes
		the task is done should be yes
</spell>
